{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779121391,"nonce":"KXPeaX6BGZIBCIeFLPWeXKeZjWo=","message":"## UX Review: seeded RLRMP example shows the current pipeline UI is metadata-first, not interaction-first\n\nContext: after loading the seeded RLRMP movement-ramp example (`d3b1163`), the user reviewed the Train, Evaluate, Analyze, and Report stages plus earlier annotations from the unpopulated state. The example data was useful because it made the same structural problem more visible: the current UI mostly prints internal pipeline metadata rather than giving the user a clear workflow for selecting, transforming, executing, inspecting, and reporting over runs.\n\n### Core problem\n\nThe bottom pane and stage sidebars currently expose backend concepts too directly: collection identifiers, repeated object roles, manifest/artifact categories, hash-like run IDs, stage kind/status tags, and duplicated labels such as train/training/eval/evaluation. This reads like a database/debug view. The product needs an interaction model centered on what the user is trying to do next, not a passive listing of stored objects.\n\n### Train-stage issues observed\n\n- The sidebar says the training input is \"None selected\", but it is not clear what a training input means. For training, the meaningful inputs may be the model/task/objective/protocol/sweep definition rather than an upstream run collection, so the UI should not reuse the same generic input language without explaining the role.\n- Training outputs showed seven completed movement-ramp runs, but each row effectively said \"training run:\" plus an identifier. The user-facing row should summarize the run variant, sweep parameters, seed/repetition, status, key metrics, and available actions. Raw hashes or manifest IDs should be hidden by default or moved into an advanced/provenance detail.\n- The Manifests section repeated the same \"training run\" labels already shown under Outputs, and the per-row source label \"RLRMP\" looked redundant because a Studio project is normally one project/source context. Show source only when multiple imported sources or projects actually matter.\n- Artifact listings exposed paths and generic roles without making clear how they are useful. \"Model checkpoints\" may belong as a train-run artifact, but \"summary figures\" may be analysis/report products rather than train outputs, or at least need clear role names and user actions.\n- Training configuration remains unclear: remote worker, cloud, local, RunPod/GCP, CPU/GPU/TPU, and disabled buttons like \"Prepare local plan\" or \"Materialize pipeline\" do not communicate prerequisites or the next action. The user should not manually act as the glue between cloud provider details and text fields unless explicitly in an advanced/manual mode.\n\n### Evaluate-stage issues observed\n\n- The main Evaluate area shows Collections with input/output counts, then Selection and Execution Draft sections, but it appears descriptive rather than interactive. The user cannot tell how to choose a subset of training runs, filter by parameter/metric/status, create a named selection, or launch an evaluation over that selection.\n- The right sidebar duplicates the same collection information and prints backend labels like `collection:training-runs`; this should either become a useful selected-object inspector or be removed/repositioned.\n- Redundant words and tags (`eval`, `Evaluate`, `Evaluation scenario`, `Evaluation runs`) consume a large fraction of visible text without increasing orientation. Stage identity is already present in the tabs and should not be repeated unless it disambiguates a concrete object.\n\n### Analyze/report issues observed\n\n- Seeded analysis nodes such as \"Peak velocity and hold drift\" appear as ad-hoc black boxes and are not visibly wired to the selected data on the left. Parameters such as `source figure` are confusing because an analysis should usually consume selected runs/evaluation outputs/artifacts, not an opaque figure object.\n- Clicking Generate Figure immediately changed to Retry, with no visible progress, error, or missing-input explanation. Execution feedback needs a real status model: queued/running/done/failed, structured error details, and a way to trace missing bindings or incompatible inputs.\n- Report could not be meaningfully reviewed because analysis generation did not produce usable products. Report UX should depend on selected analysis products/figures/tables through explicit inclusion and ordering controls, not hidden state.\n\n### Design implications\n\nThis is not a polish pass. The current Train/Evaluate/Report panes, and possibly the bottom-pane shell itself, can be redesigned. The analysis canvas visual language may be worth preserving in part because it aligns with the structural graph authoring language, but the rest of the pipeline UI should be rebuilt around interaction.\n\nThe needed mental model remains compatible with this spec's collection-transform framing:\n\n`draft/spec -\u003e run or artifact collection -\u003e filter/select/group -\u003e execute next stage -\u003e inspect outputs -\u003e use selected products downstream`\n\nBut the UI must make those verbs concrete. Every stage should answer:\n\n- What am I starting from?\n- What subset or variant set am I operating on?\n- What protocol/spec will run?\n- What is ready, invalid, running, or complete?\n- What did it produce?\n- What can I do next with those outputs?\n\n### Requirements for the next redesign slice\n\n- Replace generic input/output count blocks with interactive collection views: table/list cards with facets, checkboxes, grouping, status, key metrics, and actions.\n- Represent training runs by meaningful user-facing summaries first: variant name, changed parameters, seed/replicate, metric snippets, completion state, checkpoint availability, and selection state. Raw IDs stay available in a detail/provenance drawer.\n- Make selection a first-class object: all compatible upstream outputs by default, explicit filters/manual picks/named selections, validation when an input is incompatible, and visible downstream impact.\n- Turn the stage sidebar into either a selected-object inspector or a compact workflow/lineage summary, not a duplicate database dump.\n- Remove redundant stage labels and backend descriptors from primary UI. Use words only where they help the next action.\n- Clarify execution targets as user choices: local CPU, local accelerator if available, managed/cloud/RunPod worker, or advanced manual endpoint. Provider/server fields should be hidden until needed.\n- Disabled actions must show the blocking requirements in-place.\n- Analysis nodes must declare their required inputs and visibly bind to selected training/evaluation/artifact collections. Generation failures must show actionable errors.\n- Artifacts should be grouped by user role and action: checkpoints to resume/export/evaluate, metrics to compare/filter, figures/tables to inspect/report, logs/config/provenance to debug.\n- The seeded RLRMP example should remain a review fixture, but it should be treated as imported legacy runs. Any missing metadata should be represented honestly, with placeholders only where they support the interaction model.\n\n### Research request\n\nBefore the next implementation slice, gather UI precedents from adjacent products/patterns: ML experiment managers, data workflow tools, batch-processing UIs, scientific workflow builders, and notebook/reporting systems. The goal is to avoid designing this from scratch and to ground the next Studio redesign in proven interaction patterns for run collections, stage inputs/outputs, subset selection, execution readiness, artifact browsing, analysis generation, and report assembly.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}